From: Dmytro-K <dimattt92@googlemail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: macb: free pending TX skbs on close and link change

The driver never released frames still between tx_tail and tx_head (queued
or sent but not yet reaped):
- macb_close() -> macb_free_consistent() just kfree()d queue->tx_skb,
- macb_mac_link_up() just zeroed tx_head/tx_tail.

Besides leaking the skbs and their DMA mappings, this keeps the owning
socket's send buffer charged and never completes zerocopy ubuf_infos
(MSG_ZEROCOPY notifications, in-kernel zerocopy senders such as iqnet
waiting for their buffers back).

Add macb_tx_drop_pending(): unmap/free every pending entry with
macb_tx_unmap(bp, macb_tx_skb(queue, tail), 0), account them as
tx_dropped and reset tx_head/tx_tail. Call it:
- from macb_free_consistent() before the tx_skb array is freed, like
  mainline commit 27f575836cfe ("net: macb: drop in-flight Tx SKBs on
  close"); NAPI is disabled and the controller reset there. It runs with
  BHs disabled, so the skbs (IRQs on, budget 0: consume_skb()) and with
  them the zerocopy completions and frag page unrefs are released inside
  an RCU (BH) read-side section that a sender's synchronize_net() waits
  for, as for completions from the TX softirq;
- from macb_mac_link_down() (macb_tx_drop_all_pending()), under
  tx_ptr_lock (vs. macb_start_xmit()/macb_tx_complete()) with bp->lock
  nested inside it in macb_start_xmit()'s order (vs.
  macb_tx_error_task(), which walks the ring under bp->lock only);
- from macb_mac_link_up() instead of the bare tx_head/tx_tail reset, as a
  safety net for anything queued while the link was down, before bp->lock
  is taken.

Link down must leave nothing that can add to the ring until link up:
- macb_mac_link_down() stops the queues with netif_tx_disable() instead
  of netif_tx_stop_all_queues(): it takes each queue's xmit lock, so no
  macb_start_xmit() is still running when the ring is dropped;
- macb_tx_complete() only wakes its stopped queue while TE is set (NCR
  read under tx_ptr_lock), and macb_tx_error_task() only restarts the
  queues while TE is set (under bp->lock). macb_mac_link_down() clears
  TE under bp->lock (like every other NCR read-modify-write, so a
  concurrent TSTART write-back cannot set TE again), reads NCR back,
  then takes both locks once (a first
  macb_tx_drop_all_pending()) to wait for a TX poll or error task that
  still saw TE set, then netif_tx_disable(), then drops the ring again
  for frames an xmit still in progress may have added. So a racing NAPI
  TX poll can no longer reopen a queue during link down (the qdisc is
  only deactivated later, from linkwatch).

Clearing TE resets the controller's TX queue pointer to TBQP (ring index
0), so frames left in the ring at link down could not be sent from where
they are. Mainline instead keeps them across a link flap and rotates the
ring at link up (commit 881a0263d502 ("net: macb: Shuffle the tx ring
before enabling tx")); here they are dropped at link down so their
resources and zerocopy completions are released immediately rather than
after an unbounded link-down period, and stale real-time data is not sent
late. At link down IRQs are disabled under the locks, so the skbs go
through dev_consume_skb_any() to the NET_TX softirq and complete shortly
after.

macb_close() ordering is unchanged and consistent with this: the core
has deactivated the qdisc and waited for xmits before ndo_stop;
netif_tx_stop_all_queues() and napi_disable() come before phylink_stop()
(-> macb_mac_link_down() if the link was up), and macb_free_consistent()
runs after the controller reset. In macb_open() the queues are started
before the first link up, but with the carrier off the qdisc is not
active until phylink's link up, which drops the (empty) ring, sets TE and
wakes them.

This tree has no BQL in macb (no netdev_tx_sent_queue()), so there is no
netdev_tx_reset_queue() to add.
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/cadence/macb_main.c | 143 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--------
 1 file changed, 134 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/cadence/macb_main.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/cadence/macb_main.c
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/cadence/macb_main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/cadence/macb_main.c
@@ -746,12 +746,15 @@
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&bp->lock, flags);
 }
 
+static void macb_tx_drop_all_pending(struct macb *bp);
+
 static void macb_mac_link_down(struct phylink_config *config, unsigned int mode,
 			       phy_interface_t interface)
 {
 	struct net_device *ndev = to_net_dev(config->dev);
 	struct macb *bp = netdev_priv(ndev);
 	struct macb_queue *queue;
+	unsigned long flags;
 	unsigned int q;
 	u32 ctrl;
 
@@ -760,11 +763,44 @@
 			queue_writel(queue, IDR,
 				     bp->rx_intr_mask | MACB_TX_INT_FLAGS | MACB_BIT(HRESP));
 
-	/* Disable Rx and Tx */
+	/* Disable Rx and Tx. The NCR read-modify-write is done under
+	 * bp->lock like every other one (macb_start_xmit() sets TSTART
+	 * under it): otherwise a concurrent TSTART write-back could set TE
+	 * again and defeat the TE-gated queue wake below. The read back
+	 * makes sure the TE clear has reached the controller before another
+	 * CPU's macb_tx_complete()/macb_tx_error_task() reads NCR after
+	 * taking the locks in macb_tx_drop_all_pending().
+	 */
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&bp->lock, flags);
 	ctrl = macb_readl(bp, NCR) & ~(MACB_BIT(RE) | MACB_BIT(TE));
 	macb_writel(bp, NCR, ctrl);
+	macb_readl(bp, NCR);
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&bp->lock, flags);
+
+	if (!(bp->caps & MACB_CAPS_MACB_IS_EMAC)) {
+		/* macb_tx_complete() (under tx_ptr_lock) and
+		 * macb_tx_error_task() (under bp->lock) only reopen the TX
+		 * queues while TE is set. Taking both locks once, which
+		 * macb_tx_drop_all_pending() does, waits for one that still
+		 * saw TE set, so once netif_tx_disable() below has stopped the
+		 * queues nothing reopens them before macb_mac_link_up().
+		 */
+		macb_tx_drop_all_pending(bp);
+	}
+
+	/* Stop the queues and wait for a macb_start_xmit() in progress (it
+	 * runs under the queue's xmit lock), so none is mid-flight when the
+	 * ring is dropped below.
+	 */
+	netif_tx_disable(ndev);
 
-	netif_tx_stop_all_queues(ndev);
+	/* Frames queued while the link was up (including by an xmit that was
+	 * still running above) would sit in the ring, holding their skbs,
+	 * socket memory and zerocopy completions, until the link comes back,
+	 * and then be lost anyway: free them now.
+	 */
+	if (!(bp->caps & MACB_CAPS_MACB_IS_EMAC))
+		macb_tx_drop_all_pending(bp);
 }
 
 static void macb_mac_link_up(struct phylink_config *config,
@@ -780,6 +816,16 @@
 	unsigned int q;
 	u32 ctrl;
 
+	/* TE is off, the controller restarts from ring index 0: resync the
+	 * software ring with it. macb_mac_link_down() already emptied it and
+	 * the queues have stayed stopped since, so normally there is nothing
+	 * left; but should anything have been queued meanwhile, free it
+	 * rather than leak it by just zeroing tx_head/tx_tail. Done before
+	 * taking bp->lock, which nests inside tx_ptr_lock.
+	 */
+	if (!(bp->caps & MACB_CAPS_MACB_IS_EMAC))
+		macb_tx_drop_all_pending(bp);
+
 	spin_lock_irqsave(&bp->lock, flags);
 
 	ctrl = macb_or_gem_readl(bp, NCFGR);
@@ -804,12 +850,9 @@
 		if (rx_pause)
 			ctrl |= MACB_BIT(PAE);
 
-		for (q = 0, queue = bp->queues; q < bp->num_queues; ++q, ++queue) {
-			queue->tx_head = 0;
-			queue->tx_tail = 0;
+		for (q = 0, queue = bp->queues; q < bp->num_queues; ++q, ++queue)
 			queue_writel(queue, IER,
 				     bp->rx_intr_mask | MACB_TX_INT_FLAGS | MACB_BIT(HRESP));
-		}
 	}
 
 	macb_or_gem_writel(bp, NCFGR, ctrl);
@@ -1089,6 +1132,65 @@
 	}
 }
 
+/* Unmap and free every frame still between tx_tail and tx_head (queued, or
+ * sent but not yet reaped by macb_tx_complete()), count them as dropped and
+ * reset the ring indexes. The caller must have stopped the transmitter and
+ * either hold queue->tx_ptr_lock or have quiesced the queue (macb_close()).
+ *
+ * Freeing the skbs matters beyond the memory: until they are released,
+ * socket send buffer accounting and zerocopy (MSG_ZEROCOPY, in-kernel
+ * ubuf_info) completions are held back indefinitely.
+ */
+static void macb_tx_drop_pending(struct macb_queue *queue)
+{
+	struct macb *bp = queue->bp;
+	unsigned int dropped = 0, tail;
+
+	if (queue->tx_skb) {
+		for (tail = queue->tx_tail; tail != queue->tx_head; tail++) {
+			struct macb_tx_skb *tx_skb = macb_tx_skb(queue, tail);
+
+			if (tx_skb->skb)
+				dropped++;
+			/* budget 0: may run with IRQs disabled */
+			macb_tx_unmap(bp, tx_skb, 0);
+		}
+	}
+
+	queue->stats.tx_dropped += dropped;
+	bp->dev->stats.tx_dropped += dropped;
+
+	queue->tx_head = 0;
+	queue->tx_tail = 0;
+}
+
+/* Link change: TE has been cleared, which also resets the controller's
+ * internal TX queue pointer to TBQP (ring index 0), so the frames still in
+ * the ring would never be sent from where they are. Drop them.
+ *
+ * tx_ptr_lock excludes macb_start_xmit() and macb_tx_complete(); bp->lock,
+ * nested inside it in the same order as in macb_start_xmit(), excludes
+ * macb_tx_error_task(), which walks the ring under bp->lock only.
+ *
+ * IRQs are disabled here, so the skbs are not freed inline:
+ * dev_consume_skb_any() queues them for the NET_TX softirq, which runs
+ * their zerocopy completions shortly after.
+ */
+static void macb_tx_drop_all_pending(struct macb *bp)
+{
+	struct macb_queue *queue;
+	unsigned long flags;
+	unsigned int q;
+
+	for (q = 0, queue = bp->queues; q < bp->num_queues; ++q, ++queue) {
+		spin_lock_irqsave(&queue->tx_ptr_lock, flags);
+		spin_lock(&bp->lock);
+		macb_tx_drop_pending(queue);
+		spin_unlock(&bp->lock);
+		spin_unlock_irqrestore(&queue->tx_ptr_lock, flags);
+	}
+}
+
 static void macb_set_addr(struct macb *bp, struct macb_dma_desc *desc, dma_addr_t addr)
 {
 #ifdef CONFIG_ARCH_DMA_ADDR_T_64BIT
@@ -1236,8 +1338,12 @@
 	if (halt_timeout)
 		macb_writel(bp, NCR, macb_readl(bp, NCR) | MACB_BIT(TE));
 
-	/* Now we are ready to start transmission again */
-	netif_tx_start_all_queues(bp->dev);
+	/* Now we are ready to start transmission again; but not during a link
+	 * down (TE off, see macb_mac_link_down()): macb_mac_link_up() wakes
+	 * the queues then.
+	 */
+	if (macb_readl(bp, NCR) & MACB_BIT(TE))
+		netif_tx_start_all_queues(bp->dev);
 	macb_writel(bp, NCR, macb_readl(bp, NCR) | MACB_BIT(TSTART));
 
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&bp->lock, flags);
@@ -1338,9 +1444,15 @@
 	}
 
 	queue->tx_tail = tail;
+	/* Not while TE is off: macb_mac_link_down() has stopped the queues
+	 * and dropped the ring, and a TX poll still running then must not
+	 * reopen them before macb_mac_link_up() (NCR is read under
+	 * tx_ptr_lock, which macb_mac_link_down() takes after clearing TE).
+	 */
 	if (__netif_subqueue_stopped(bp->dev, queue_index) &&
 	    CIRC_CNT(queue->tx_head, queue->tx_tail,
-		     bp->tx_ring_size) <= MACB_TX_WAKEUP_THRESH(bp))
+		     bp->tx_ring_size) <= MACB_TX_WAKEUP_THRESH(bp) &&
+	    (macb_readl(bp, NCR) & MACB_BIT(TE)))
 		netif_wake_subqueue(bp->dev, queue_index);
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&queue->tx_ptr_lock, flags);
 
@@ -2552,6 +2664,19 @@
 	bp->macbgem_ops.mog_free_rx_buffers(bp);
 
 	for (q = 0, queue = bp->queues; q < bp->num_queues; ++q, ++queue) {
+		/* Free the frames still in flight (macb_close(): NAPI is
+		 * disabled and the controller reset, no locking needed). The
+		 * open/alloc error paths come here with an empty ring.
+		 *
+		 * With BHs disabled the skbs are freed right here (budget 0,
+		 * IRQs on: consume_skb()), so their zerocopy completions and
+		 * frag page unrefs run inside an RCU (BH) read-side section:
+		 * a zerocopy sender's synchronize_net() then also waits for
+		 * them, as for completions from the TX softirq.
+		 */
+		local_bh_disable();
+		macb_tx_drop_pending(queue);
+		local_bh_enable();
 		kfree(queue->tx_skb);
 		queue->tx_skb = NULL;
 		if (queue->tx_ring) {
